---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  type="article"
  title="Why DNS changes need a manual apply"
  description="Why DNS changes for bvrinfra.in require a manual apply in HCP Terraform instead of auto-applying on merge."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="kind">DNS · published 8 October 2026</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Why DNS changes need a manual apply</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Managing DNS as code prevents silent drift and forgotten records.
        For bvrinfra.in, every DNS change goes through a pull request and a speculative plan,
        but live updates wait for a human to confirm and apply them. Here is why.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-22-stale-records">
      <h2 id="the-22-stale-records">The 22 stale records</h2>
      <p>
        When I started the rebuild in October 2026, after the <a href="/writing/september-2026-outage/">September outage</a>, the DNS zone for bvrinfra.in still held 22 stale records.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seventeen of those records pointed to a deleted Cloudflare Tunnel.
        One pointed to a private address on my home network.
        The remaining records pointed to a deleted virtual machine or projects that had ended.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of these records caused an immediate incident on their own,
        but together they represented unmanaged drift accumulated over time through hand edits in the web dashboard.
        There was no recorded intent, no review process, and no easy way to know if a record was still needed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-as-code">
      <h2 id="dns-as-code">DNS as code with Terraform</h2>
      <p>
        To fix this, I imported the DNS records into Terraform using the Cloudflare provider,
        with state stored in HCP Terraform under the <code>bvrinfra-dns</code> workspace.
        The dashboard is now considered read-only by convention.
      </p>
      <p>
        All zone records are defined in <a href="https://github.com/BODAPATI88/bvrinfra/blob/main/infra/terraform/main.tf"><code>infra/terraform/main.tf</code></a> inside a <code>local.records</code> map.
        To prevent accidental record deletions, every record resource includes a <code>prevent_destroy = true</code> lifecycle guardrail.
        Deleting a record requires a pull request that explicitly removes that guard first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-workflow">
      <h2 id="the-workflow">The plan-on-PR workflow</h2>
      <p>
        When a DNS change is needed, the process follows three distinct stages:
      </p>
      <ol class="workflow">
        <li>
          <strong>Pull request:</strong> A branch updates <code>infra/terraform/main.tf</code>.
          GitHub Actions checks syntax and formatting without holding production credentials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Speculative plan:</strong> HCP Terraform automatically runs a speculative plan against live Cloudflare DNS
          and links the result from the pull request's checks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Merge and queue:</strong> Merging the PR triggers a production plan on <code>main</code> in HCP Terraform,
          which pauses and waits for manual approval.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="why-manual-apply">
      <h2 id="why-manual-apply">Why auto-apply is turned off</h2>
      <p>
        Auto-applying infrastructure changes on merge can be risky when automated tools or AI assistants draft code.
        Under ADR 0001 (D7), AI agents work strictly in the build loop to draft pull requests,
        and no automated tool holds credentials to apply live infrastructure changes unattended.
      </p>
      <p>
        Requiring a human operator to log into HCP Terraform, review the queued plan against the merged PR,
        and click <strong>Confirm & apply</strong> provides a deliberate circuit breaker.
        It means no change reaches Cloudflare through Terraform until I have read the plan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="summary">
      <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
      <p>
        Hand edits caused 22 stale records.
        Terraform enforces structure and intent in code,
        and manual apply means every DNS change through Terraform waits for my sign-off.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 46rem; }
  .post header { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .kind { color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .post section { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .post h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1.1rem + 0.8vw, 1.75rem);
    font-stretch: 92%;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .post p, .post li { line-height: 1.65; }
  .post p { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .post ol.workflow { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .workflow li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .workflow li::marker { color: var(--muted); }
</style>
